{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 15, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterfully crafted film that delves into the birth of facebook and the complex, often bitter relationships that surrounded its creation. directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, the movie is a gripping blend of drama, tension, and sharp dialogue that feels as electric as the early days of the internet itself. jesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, capturing his brilliance, social awkwardness, and simmering resentment with uncanny precision. his portrayal isn't sympathetic in the traditional sense, but it’s deeply human — a genius haunted by exclusion and driven by a need to prove himself.\n\nthe supporting cast is equally stellar. andrew garfield brings vulnerability and quiet heartbreak as justin timberlake’s portrayal of sean parker, the charismatic, reckless co-founder of napster who becomes zuckerberg’s mentor and eventual rival. timberlake, in a surprising turn, exudes a dangerous charm that both seduces and destabilizes. jesse plemons and armie hammer round out the ensemble as the winklevoss twins, their synchronized performances a technical marvel that underscores the film’s themes of identity and betrayal.\n\nsorkin’s script is a triumph — rapid-fire, witty, and layered with legal and emotional stakes. the courtroom scenes, framed as flashbacks, provide a narrative structure that mirrors the film’s central conflict: who owns an idea? who deserves credit? the pacing never falters, and every line feels deliberate, every silence loaded. the score by trent reznor and atticus ross is haunting and minimalist, using electronic tones to evoke the coldness of technology and the isolation of its creators.\n\nfincher’s direction is immaculate. every frame is composed with clinical precision, from the dimly lit dorm rooms of harvard to the sterile boardrooms of venture capitalists. the use of light and shadow reflects the moral ambiguity of the characters. even the mundane act of typing on a keyboard becomes cinematic, charged with tension.\n\nbeneath the surface, the film isn’t really about facebook — it’s about ambition, friendship, betrayal, and the cost of innovation in a digital age. it asks whether success can ever be pure when it’s built on broken trust. the final scene, where zuckerberg refreshes a facebook page over and over, hoping for a response from his ex-girlfriend, is devastating in its quiet loneliness — a perfect encapsulation of the movie’s central tragedy: even when you build the world’s largest social network, you might still be utterly alone.\n\nthe social network is more than a biopic; it’s a modern american fable, brilliantly acted, sharply written, and hauntingly resonant. it doesn’t glorify its subject — it dissects him. and in doing so, it holds up a mirror to our own digital lives.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00014733182615600526}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -1.9073468138230965e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -9.536738616588991e-07}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -2.264974000354414e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0005540504935197532}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.4593120217323303}, {"token": "fully", "logprob": -0.4984855651855469}, {"token": " crafted", "logprob": -0.0008233971311710775}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.005141132976859808}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -9.226373367710039e-05}, {"token": " del", "logprob": -1.2677857875823975}, {"token": "ves", "logprob": -3.4570634852570947e-06}, {"token": " into", "logprob": -0.00863284058868885}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " birth", "logprob": -2.49967098236084}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00014101465058047324}, {"token": " facebook", "logprob": -0.0003665013937279582}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.01819128356873989}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -4.6491513785440475e-06}, {"